so this recent paper has got quite a lot  of attention and it includes a lot of  words you hear a lot on this channel  it's analytics and lifespan so i thought  i would read the paper  plus i was also lucky to hear toru  minamino present this data at the  senescence conference last week  which i unfortunately had to watch from  the comfort of my home instead of osaka  so hello and welcome to the shiki  science show where in this video i will  break down the results and discuss  whether we have a faxing now for aging  so xenolytics then  these commonly refer to drugs that  selectively kill senescent cells  cells that accumulate in the body with  age and through their inflammatory  phenotype are thought to also contribute  to aging  however i think now's analytics are more  generally referred to as any strategy  that can selectively kill these  senescent cells and leave your healthy  happy cells happy and healthy  only last week did i inform you on a  recently discovered cinelytic  procyonidin c1 found in grape seed  extracts so another drug-based approach  but another approach that i want to  remind you of was this antibody drug  conjugate  here if you remember they targeted  senescent cells by having antibodies  recognize membrane proteins expressed on  the membranes of senescent cells  and these antibodies are conjugated to a  cytotoxic drug  such that when they recognize the  membrane protein and are subsequently  uptaken by the senescent cell they cause  the senescent cell to die  now whilst the membrane marker used in  this study may not be the best choice  if more selective proteins can be chosen  it will help to reduce negative and  unknown impacts on healthy cells  which would still be targeted by the use  of synalytic small molecules like  pro-cyanide and c1  so this now brings us on to the recent  publication  sonolytic vaccination improves normal  and pathological age-related phenotypes  and increases lifespan in prodroid mice  so how does this strategy work  well firstly they chose a different  membrane target  here they selected gpnmb a glycoprotein  that is upregulated in vascular cells in  patients and mice with atherosclerosis  and the expression of gpnb  is correlated with other senescence  markers so the authors first developed  antibodies that could recognize the  protein gpnb and then they could give  these to the mice as a vaccine as the  antibodies can activate the immune cells  to then kill cells that express the  protein gp and mb so similarly to how  vaccines that recognize antigens  expressed on cells that come from  ferreters or bacteria that your immune  system can use to kill infected cells  it's just this time the marker is gpnb  which is expressed on some senescent  cells  so how did they test their vaccine well  first they fed mice are high fat diet  from four weeks of age which increases  the abundance of gp and mb which is  expressed along with these other  senescence markers then the mice were  vaccinated against gpnb at eight weeks  of age and analyzed eight weeks later  and while it seemed to work it reduced  the signal of gpnb along with the  reduction of sa beta gal which is used  to mark senescent cells here you can see  in adipose tissue and to also improve  the metabolic profile shown here in the  glucose and insulin tolerance test  so you might be thinking well how does  this compare to other synalytics well  you're in luck because the authors also  thought that  again feeding rice are high-fat diets  instead of giving them the vaccine at  eight weeks they gave mice satanism and  quercetin for five consecutive days  at eight weeks and also again at 12  weeks of age and they did the same thing  with another synolitic no fetal arcs  in all cases they saw a reduction in  tennessean staining and visceral adipose  tissue at 16 weeks of age  however it was only in the vaccinated  mice that these beneficial effects were  maintained at 24 weeks of age  so the key difference seems to be in  permanency once you've got immunized  with the senolitic vaccine the body  continues to recognize the expression of  the synolytic marker gpnb  but due to these stronger and more  permanent impacts on the organism i  suspect it will take longer to see  something like a vaccine developed for  humans as is potentially the risk for  autoimmune diseases recognizing a  protein that our body makes but there is  also the argument that you could just  keep giving the mice this hit-and-run  strategy with this analytics  at more time points than was tested in  the study  so what about longevity well to  investigate the vaccine on lifespan they  then vaccinated a progeria mouse model  so here the mice have a mutation such  that they age faster and compared with  control mice vaccinated at 10 weeks of  age showed a better survival rate albeit  it was more significant in the male mice  than the female mice  so all in all i think this was an  interesting study though i don't think  i'd get too excited just yet  there are many other ways to induce  senescence and many other tissues that  weren't examined in this study  there doesn't appear to be much talk  about translating this to humans yet  either which i'm not really surprised  about  i think it's more of a proof of  principle but it demonstrates the  variety of strategies that are being  developed so that's pretty cool  so anyway i hope you've learned  something in this quite brief video  thank you to my patreon supporters and  thank you for listening  you
 

